1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,300
A bigger number does not automatically tell us what a player did well.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:09,904
We will compare points and cleared lines in Tetris, then see how a difference

3
00:00:09,904 --> 00:00:12,420
from an opponent changes what a total means.

4
00:00:12,670 --> 00:00:16,287
Finally, Balatro will help us connect visible calculation feedback to names

5
00:00:16,287 --> 00:00:18,940
and presentations that make performance easier to read.

6
00:00:18,967 --> 00:00:23,487
Tetris Effect: Connected displays points and cleared lines in separate fields.

7
00:00:24,007 --> 00:00:29,005
A placement can change points while the line count stays still. Clearing a row

8
00:00:29,005 --> 00:00:30,607
can change both displays.

9
00:00:30,912 --> 00:00:35,333
First, follow the line counter. As new pieces are placed, look for a moment

10
00:00:35,333 --> 00:00:38,752
when the line count stays unchanged while the score moves.

11
00:00:41,222 --> 00:00:46,422
Treating progress and evaluation as one quantity would hide this distinction.

12
00:00:47,967 --> 00:00:50,627
Does the same number of cleared lines have to mean the same score?

13
00:00:50,847 --> 00:00:55,294
Here, both players reach an equal line count but have different point totals.

14
00:00:55,294 --> 00:00:58,067
Equal quantities do not imply equal evaluations.

15
00:01:03,007 --> 00:01:07,246
This time, compare the two line counts first. Find a moment when they match,

16
00:01:07,246 --> 00:01:11,207
then move your attention to the scores and the difference between them.

17
00:01:13,082 --> 00:01:17,842
Start a design by separating what you count from what you choose to evaluate.

18
00:01:19,967 --> 00:01:25,187
A scoring rule expresses a choice about which kinds of play deserve more
value.

19
00:01:25,467 --> 00:01:31,078
The visible T-SPIN DOUBLE and BACK-TO-BACK notices describe the action more

20
00:01:31,078 --> 00:01:33,847
specifically than a line count alone.

21
00:01:34,107 --> 00:01:38,080
Read the action label that appears with the moving pieces. The notice draws

22
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:41,947
attention to what just happened, while the score shows a retained result.

23
00:01:41,947 --> 00:01:45,619
The clip does not expose the complete scoring table. In our own design,

24
00:01:45,619 --> 00:01:48,827
however, the action we value and its score label should agree.

25
00:01:49,217 --> 00:01:53,857
A running total alone can make the latest contribution hard to identify.

26
00:01:54,177 --> 00:01:58,374
Watch the event notice as rows clear and the point display changes. One

27
00:01:58,374 --> 00:02:02,157
describes this action; the other retains the accumulated result.

28
00:02:02,412 --> 00:02:06,958
Separate the moment when the notice appears from the view after it passes. The

29
00:02:06,958 --> 00:02:10,572
total stays available for comparison after the notice is gone.

30
00:02:10,617 --> 00:02:16,797
Keeping both readable helps a new contribution remain understandable inside a
large total.

31
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:20,840
A large total and a lead over an opponent answer different questions.

32
00:02:31,183 --> 00:02:35,290
At the observed moment when both line counts are twenty-seven, the left score

33
00:02:35,290 --> 00:02:37,423
is 14,096 and the right score is 17,016.

34
00:02:37,468 --> 00:02:41,748
When both line counts show 27, the displayed scores differ by 2920.

35
00:02:48,483 --> 00:02:52,851
In the later shot, the left player has fewer cleared lines but a higher score.

36
00:02:52,851 --> 00:02:55,203
That lead is a state observed during play.

37
00:02:55,698 --> 00:02:59,918
In a later shot, the player with fewer cleared lines leads by 141 points.

38
00:03:00,153 --> 00:03:04,862
A signed difference makes the comparison reference explicit. A lead during

39
00:03:04,862 --> 00:03:07,853
play still does not establish the final winner.

40
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:15,900
A label tells the player which meaning to attach to a number.

41
00:03:16,015 --> 00:03:20,103
First decide whether to read the score or the number of lines. A number

42
00:03:20,103 --> 00:03:23,615
without a name makes it hard to choose which result improved.

43
00:03:26,745 --> 00:03:29,984
Just as points and lines differ here, recovered items and an overall

44
00:03:29,984 --> 00:03:33,365
evaluation would communicate different things in a hypothetical design.

45
00:03:33,685 --> 00:03:38,351
Specify what is measured, its unit, and whether a higher or lower value

46
00:03:38,351 --> 00:03:40,125
represents a better result.

47
00:03:40,150 --> 00:03:45,190
These short Balatro closeups draw attention to the selected cards, hand name,

48
00:03:45,190 --> 00:03:46,630
chips, and multiplier.

49
00:03:47,208 --> 00:03:51,003
After the card reactions, numbers grow and receive emphasis. The result is

50
00:03:51,003 --> 00:03:54,848
presented through visible changes rather than only as a static final value.

51
00:03:55,268 --> 00:04:00,872
A design can reveal important contributions before its total. As the animation

52
00:04:00,872 --> 00:04:04,608
expands, keep the number and its meaning unobscured.

53
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:09,140
If every detail receives the same visual weight, the useful result becomes
harder to find.

54
00:04:09,420 --> 00:04:13,470
During play, totals and line counts remain available while individual event

55
00:04:13,470 --> 00:04:16,980
notices appear and disappear. They serve different reading times.

56
00:04:17,150 --> 00:04:21,598
Pieces keep falling, while the places to find the total and line count remain

57
00:04:21,598 --> 00:04:25,410
stable. Look at those locations separately from the brief notices.

58
00:04:25,820 --> 00:04:30,969
Keep values used for repeated comparison in stable positions and distinguish

59
00:04:30,969 --> 00:04:34,560
brief event emphasis without displacing those values.

60
00:04:36,333 --> 00:04:40,165
Before adding a score, ask what it measures, what it is compared with, and

61
00:04:40,165 --> 00:04:41,873
whether its changes are readable.

62
00:04:42,073 --> 00:04:46,342
Tetris showed quantity versus evaluation and a live comparison; Balatro showed

63
00:04:46,342 --> 00:04:49,625
calculation feedback. Meaningful performance comes before an

64
00:04:49,625 --> 00:04:50,993
impressive-looking total.

65
00:04:51,183 --> 00:04:55,214
Read the score’s name, the comparison between players, and the moment when a

66
00:04:55,214 --> 00:04:59,403
value changes, in that order. These are different questions about the same
play.

67
00:04:59,673 --> 00:05:03,121
Try explaining the labels and baselines in your own interface. For guided

68
00:05:03,121 --> 00:05:07,513
game-programming design and implementation, the description links to our
coaching information.
